<BaseLayout title="CV12 (Zhongwan) — Stomach Center Point" description="Everything you need to self-treat CV12 (Zhongwan) — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "CV12 (Zhongwan)", href: "/points/cv12-zhongwan-acupressure-point" }]} datePublished="2026-08-18" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">CV12 (Zhongwan) — Stomach Center</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">CV12, known as Zhongwan or Middle Cavity, is the classic stomach center point. On the midline halfway between the sternum and the navel, it is used for nausea, bloating, indigestion, and an unsettled stomach.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Conception Vessel (CV / Ren Mai)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Upper abdomen, on the midline halfway between the bottom of the sternum and the navel</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Nausea, bloating, indigestion, stomach upset, fullness and heaviness after meals</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Easy — a comfortable, easy-to-find upper-midline point</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–2 minutes per session, in slow circles</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–2 times daily, at least 30–60 minutes after meals</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">ST36 (Zusanli)</a>, <a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">PC6 (Neiguan)</a>, <a href="/points/cv6-qihai-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">CV6 (Qihai)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Pregnancy, recent abdominal surgery, or within 30–60 minutes of a heavy meal</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why CV12 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">CV12 sits on the midline of the abdomen, on the Conception Vessel (Ren Mai), roughly halfway between the upside-down V of the ribcage and the belly button. In the traditional model, points here regulate the smooth flow of qi for the stomach and intestines. Because it sits directly over the stomach, CV12 is the point reached for when a meal leaves the belly bloated, heavy, or queasy — the abdomen holds the points used most often for bloating, indigestion, constipation, and nausea.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">In traditional Chinese medicine, nausea is understood as rebellious qi — stomach energy flowing upward instead of downward — and stomach-area points such as CV12 help restore downward flow. On a practical level, the point usually feels tender or full when pressed, which makes it easy to self-locate and satisfying to release. It pairs naturally with PC6 on the inner wrist for nausea and with ST36 below the knee, the site's master point for digestive health.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/cv12-zhongwan-location.webp" alt="CV12 (Zhongwan) acupressure point location on the upper abdomen" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm mb-6" width="414" height="276" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">CV12 is on the midline of the abdomen, about halfway between the bottom of the sternum and the navel. Feel for the upside-down V of the ribcage where the ribs meet, then measure roughly halfway down to the belly button along the midline. It lies on the Conception Vessel, over the stomach. The point usually feels tender or full when pressed.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) it sits on the exact midline, not off to either side; (2) it is roughly halfway between the bottom of the sternum and the navel; (3) it feels tender, full, or slightly heavier than the surrounding skin when pressed.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use CV12 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 dark:border-emerald-900/40 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You feel nauseous, queasy, or carsick</li>
          <li>• You feel bloated, heavy, or full after a meal</li>
          <li>• Digestion feels sluggish and the upper abdomen is uncomfortable</li>
          <li>• You want a daily stomach-maintenance point for a sensitive belly</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You are pregnant — direct abdominal pressure is generally avoided then</li>
          <li>• You have just eaten a heavy meal (wait 30–60 minutes)</li>
          <li>• You have had recent abdominal surgery or have a hernia in the area</li>
          <li>• The pain is severe, persistent, or unexplained</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: CV12 is a gentle stomach-settling practice for everyday queasiness, not a treatment for severe or acute abdominal problems.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Nausea:</strong> CV12 is a classic point for queasiness and an unsettled stomach, and it combines well with PC6 on the inner wrist.</li>
      <li><strong>Bloating and gas:</strong> Gentle pressure helps relieve distension and heaviness in the upper abdomen.</li>
      <li><strong>Indigestion:</strong> Traditionally used for sluggish digestion and abdominal discomfort after meals.</li>
      <li><strong>Stomach comfort:</strong> A daily maintenance point for people with a sensitive or easily upset stomach.</li>
      <li><strong>Restoring downward flow:</strong> In TCM, stomach-area points help restore the downward flow of qi that keeps digestion moving.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage CV12</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Wait 30 to 60 minutes after eating and avoid pressing over surgical scars. Keep the pressure gentle, warm, and matched to your breathing.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Lie on your back with your abdomen relaxed and uncovered.</li>
      <li>Place two or three fingertips on CV12 in the upper midline.</li>
      <li>Apply gentle, warm pressure in a clockwise circular motion.</li>
      <li>Breathe deeply into your belly for 2–3 minutes.</li>
      <li>Repeat once or twice daily — the constipation guide begins with a three-minute clockwise abdominal massage on waking.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing on a full stomach or digging in hard. CV12 responds to light, slow, breath-matched circles on an empty-to-settled belly — never press hard enough to feel sharp pain.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine CV12 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A stomach routine is more effective than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 1–2 minutes each.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Nausea / queasiness</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">PC6</a> → CV12 → <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">ST36</a>, holding each 1–2 min as symptoms settle</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Bloating after meals</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV12 → ST36 → PC6, after waiting 30–60 minutes past the meal</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Indigestion / heaviness</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV12 → ST36 → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4</a>, 1–2 min each, once or twice daily</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Slow digestion / maintenance</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV12 → <a href="/points/cv6-qihai-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">CV6</a> → ST36 in the morning, as a daily digestive routine</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For nausea routines, see our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-nausea" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for nausea</a> page, and explore related spots in the <a href="/body/abdomen-acupressure-points" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">abdomen acupressure points</a> body guide.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">The point may feel tender or full, and most people notice the upper belly feels looser and less heavy within a few minutes of slow circular pressure. Do not expect a stubborn bout of nausea to vanish after one press.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 1–2 sessions per day, timed after meals, bloating and post-meal heaviness often ease noticeably. This matches the pattern in studies of abdominal acupressure for digestive discomfort, where benefits appear after several days of regular use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For chronic indigestion, pair CV12 with meal timing and a regular eating pattern. Acupressure is a complement, not a replacement — persistent pain, unexplained weight loss, or blood in the stool should always be checked by a clinician rather than managed with acupressure alone.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Evidence on acupressure for digestive complaints has grown in recent years. Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure as an adjunct for nausea, vomiting, and other digestive symptoms, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. PC6, which pairs with CV12 for nausea, is among the most-studied acupressure points in this area, and midline abdominal points appear frequently in digestive research protocols.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Researchers consistently note that acupressure trials are hard to blind, because the treatment is physical and participants can usually tell whether they are being treated. This makes it difficult to separate the specific effects of a particular point from the general benefits of touch, pressure, and relaxation. The overall picture — which we discuss in detail on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work">does acupressure work?</a> review — is one of encouraging signals alongside a clear need for larger, higher-quality studies before firm conclusions can be drawn. For the best-supported nausea routine, see our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-nausea" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for nausea</a> guide.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["lee-frazier-2011"]} note="The review above evaluated acupressure for symptom management, including digestive complaints. It is not a study of CV12 in isolation." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Avoid during pregnancy:</strong> Direct pressure on the abdomen is generally avoided during pregnancy.</li>
      <li>Wait 30–60 minutes after a heavy meal, and do not apply pressure over a full bladder.</li>
      <li>Avoid if you have abdominal surgery scars or hernias in the area.</li>
      <li>Skip the point if there is skin irritation, a rash, or an open wound on the upper abdomen.</li>
      <li>Persistent pain, unexplained weight loss, or blood in the stool should be checked by a clinician.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Severe or worsening abdominal pain that is not relieved by rest</li>
        <li>Blood in vomit or stool, or black, tarry stools</li>
        <li>Persistent vomiting, or the inability to keep down fluids</li>
        <li>Unexplained weight loss alongside digestive symptoms</li>
        <li>Nausea or digestive problems that have not improved after 2–3 weeks of regular acupressure</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do I find CV12 on myself?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Find the upside-down V of the ribcage where the ribs meet, then measure roughly halfway down the midline to the belly button. The point usually feels tender or full when pressed.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press CV12?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Thirty to sixty seconds per point is a good start, one to two minutes for a deeper feel. Press at roughly 90 degrees to the skin, deepen slowly, and aim for a deep, dull deqi response, never sharp.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When is the best time to press CV12?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Wait 30–60 minutes after a meal, then press once or twice daily — in the morning on waking or after dinner both work well. For queasiness, press as soon as you feel symptoms, together with PC6 on the inner wrist.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is CV12 safe during pregnancy?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">During pregnancy, avoid direct pressure on the abdomen, and note that the site also cautions LI4, SP6, BL60, and GB21. Midline abdominal points are classically treated with extra care, so ask a practitioner or midwife first.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead of using CV12?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor for severe or persistent abdominal pain, blood in vomit or stool, unexplained weight loss, or persistent vomiting. CV12 is a gentle supportive practice for everyday queasiness — not a substitute for medical care when these warning signs appear.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-10 text-center">
      <a href="/points" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-emerald-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-emerald-400">Back to All Points</a>
